WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:10.590
October 4th is National Taco Day! Tacos
are small, cheap, and easy to assemble. And

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:14.130
they're delicious! They're the perfect
food to get from a food truck.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:18.660
Who doesn't love a taco truck? Well,
restaurants and regulators, that's who. As

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:22.920
food trucks have exploded in popularity
over the past decade, they've encountered

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.150
regulatory roadblocks in dozens of
cities. Some regulations are sensible

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.590
rules about food safety, but some cities have
barred food trucks from parking or

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.399
consign them to hidden corners of the
city. Others have banned them outright!

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:39.840
These regulations have nothing to do
with protecting consumers, and everything

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.730
to do with crushing competition. In DC,
Chicago, El Paso, and elsewhere, the

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.469
restaurant lobby has fought to curb or
restrict food trucks. The lobbyists

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:52.890
sometimes admitted their
anti-competitive intentions, but often

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.750
they tossed out bogus arguments about
sidewalk safety or the like. This is a

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.010
hidden aspect of regulations — always
pushed in the name of protecting the

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:08.310
public, they often serve most to protect
special interests from competition.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:12.180
Do you think food trucks have an unfair
advantage over restaurants? Let us know

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.990
in our poll. Also, let us know what other
topics you'd like our scholars to cover

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:20.490
in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and
subscribe for more research and more

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.720
videos from a AEI.

